## Essential governance

- Start from an explicit Task Packet: objective, allowed scope, acceptance criteria,
  risk, validation and authorized actions. Read project facts before changing files.
- Project rules may strengthen this baseline; never silently weaken either policy.
- Separate planning, implementation, independent review, quality checks and triage.
  The implementer does not approve their own work. Role profiles do not enforce gates.
- Run the relevant deterministic checks before independent review.
- Review an immutable commit: Full Review → Triage → Fix confirmed finding IDs →
  Delta Review of those fixes and their direct regressions. Keep rejected findings
  with reasons. A broader scope or changed trust boundary requires a new Full Review.
- Treat changes to governance, agents, templates, validators and workflows as HIGH
  risk. Assess downstream effects before recommending adoption.
- Record real commands, results, commit IDs and reviewer identity. Never invent
  checks, approvals, findings or completed runtime integrations.
- Require human approval for merge and release. Never publish a new baseline or
  change repository protections as a side effect of implementation.
- Keep secrets out of files and logs. Preserve existing stronger governance and
  unrelated work; stop and report conflicts rather than overwrite them.

